pine lab measures 6 V and 2 A across a resistor. Which quantity is 3 Ω?","answer":"choice-3","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Power","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.3.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Charge","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.3.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Frequency","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.3.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Resistance"}]},{"id":"physics.electricity.m.004","deckId":"physics.electricity","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A 2 Ω resistor and a 3 Ω resistor are connected in one loop. How are their resistances combined?","answer":"choice-0","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Add the resistances"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Multiply then stop","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.4.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Use only the smaller resistance","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.4.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Subtract the resistances","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.4.3"}]},{"id":"physics.electricity.m.005","deckId":"physics.electricity","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A new conducting branch is added beside an existing resistor. What happens to the equivalent resistance?","answer":"choice-1","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"It becomes negative","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.5.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"It decreases"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"It increases","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.5.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"It must stay unchanged","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.5.2"}]},{"id":"physics.electricity.m.006","deckId":"physics.electricity","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"Two bulbs connect to the same two points on a battery. Which quantity is the same across both bulbs?","answer":"choice-2","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Current in every branch","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.6.2"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Bulb power by definition","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.6.3"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Voltage"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Resistance by definition","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.6.1"}]},{"id":"physics.electricity.m.007","deckId":"physics.electricity","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A switch breaks the only conducting path to a bulb. What happens to the current?","answer":"choice-3","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Current doubles everywhere","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.7.1"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"The battery loses all mass","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.7.2"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Resistance becomes negative","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.7.3"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Current stops in the loop"}]},{"id":"physics.electricity.m.008","deckId":"physics.electricity","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A compass near a current-carrying wire turns. What field explains this?","answer":"choice-0","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Magnetic field"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"Only a gravity field change","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.8.1"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"No field of any kind","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.8.2"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Only visible light","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.8.3"}]},{"id":"physics.electricity.m.009","deckId":"physics.electricity","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A student adds more turns to a coil while keeping the current the same. What result is expected?","answer":"choice-1","options":[{"id":"choice-0","text":"Cutting the circuit open","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.9.3"},{"id":"choice-1","text":"More coil turns"},{"id":"choice-2","text":"Removing all current","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.9.1"},{"id":"choice-3","text":"Using fewer turns only","misconception":"physics.electricity.mixup.9.2"}]},{"id":"physics.electricity.m.010","deckId":"physics.electricity","level":"medium","type":"mcq","prompt":"A circuit needs a metal wire that carries current well. Which material is a commo